# Shared Responsibility Model — my notes

## Cloud computing — the basics
Computing services (compute, storage, network, AI) delivered remotely instead of
on my own premises. Key traits: on-demand, pay-as-you-go.

## Deployment models
- **Private cloud**: on-prem infra, fully my responsibility (cost + security).
- **Public cloud**: provider manages hardware/infra, I manage the rest depending
  on service model (IaaS/PaaS/SaaS).
- **Hybrid cloud**: mix of private + public, connected together (e.g. VPN /
  ExpressRoute). Example: Entra Connect syncing our on-prem AD to Azure AD.
  Hybrid is about WHERE infra physically sits — not about who secures what.

## Service models (IaaS / PaaS / SaaS)
Responsibility shifts along a spectrum — as you move from IaaS to SaaS,
the provider takes on more, I take on less.

- **IaaS**: provider gives raw infra (VMs, storage, network). I manage OS,
  patching, apps, data.
  - *My examples*: SIEM VM (I patch the OS myself), our other exi VMs.
- **PaaS**: provider gives infra + OS + runtime. I manage only my app + data.
  - *Example I don't have yet*: Azure Sentinel, Azure SQL Database — if we
    move to these, provider patches the underlying platform.
- **SaaS**: provider manages everything (infra, OS, app, updates). I just use
  it and own my data.
  - *My example*: Exchange Online / Microsoft 365.

## Shared Responsibility Model (separate from deployment model!)
Applies to any cloud service, regardless of IaaS/PaaS/SaaS or deployment model.
Splits into:
- **Security OF the cloud** (Microsoft, always): physical datacenters,
  hardware, network infra, physical security.
- **Security IN the cloud** (me, always): my data, identity/access management,
  device security.
- **The middle layer** (OS, network controls, apps) shifts depending on
  IaaS/PaaS/SaaS — more mine in IaaS, more Microsoft's in SaaS.

Rule to not confuse this with hybrid cloud: shared responsibility answers
"who secures which layer" — hybrid answers "where does the infra physically live."

